{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 22, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III became count at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna ruled as regent until he came of age. In 1174 he married Echive of Bures who was the widow of William of Montferrat and mother of Baldwin V of Jerusalem.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\n**Early life and reign**\nRaymond III spent much of his early life in Antioch under the protection of his cousin Prince Bohemond III. He was held hostage by the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Komnenos in 1165 after Bohemond was captured by Nur ad-Din. Raymond was released in 1170 after Bohemond paid a ransom. Upon returning to Tripoli he took full control of the county in 1174 following his mother’s death. Raymond III strengthened Tripoli’s defenses and maintained good relations with the Byzantine Empire. He also played a key role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem often mediating between factions.\n\n**Conflict with the Zengids**\nRaymond faced constant pressure from the Zengid dynasty led by Nur ad-Din and later Saladin. In 1175 Saladin besieged Tripoli but was forced to retreat due to a lack of supplies and reinforcements. Raymond allied with the Knights Templar and Hospitaller to defend the county. Despite these efforts Saladin continued to threaten Tripoli forcing Raymond to seek help from the Byzantine Empire and the Italian maritime republics. In 1180 he signed a truce with Saladin to buy time for Tripoli.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\n**Succession crisis and death**\nIn 1185 Raymond was chosen as regent for the young King Baldwin V of Jerusalem. He ruled alongside Queen Maria Comnena and the Ibelin faction. Raymond opposed Guy of Lusignan’s claim to the throne. When Baldwin V died in 1186 Raymond backed Humphrey IV of Toron over Guy. This led to a factional split in the kingdom. Raymond temporarily reconciled with Guy but tensions remained high. He died in 1187 likely from illness or poisoning. His death left Tripoli vulnerable to Saladin who soon after conquered the county in 1188.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n\nRaymond III’s leadership was crucial in maintaining Tripoli’s independence during a turbulent period. His political maneuvering and alliances shaped the crusader states’ fate. Though his death marked the end of Tripoli’s autonomy it underscored his legacy as a key figure in the crusades.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.17142000794410706}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.3436982333660126}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.00010549465514486656}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.008071660995483398}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -8.83301836438477e-05}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -1.1444026313256472e-05}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -0.4534485936164856}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0891289934515953}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -3.981510963058099e-05}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.000102037942269817}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.32719123363494873}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.23097148537635803}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -4.887569048150908e-06}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0007446615491062403}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0003634030872490257}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.2397689715726301e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.00012587709352374077}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.0008293526479974389}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.00015770144818816334}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.5232008695602417}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.018272755667567253}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -0.0001225396408699453}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.003869072301313281}, {"token": " 